diagnosis_text:
The fabric is healthy; leaf_router_0_1’s uplinks are not down. Both uplink interfaces on leaf_router_0_1 (eth0 and eth1) are up with link detected, and BGP is established to both spines. Specifically, eth0 (172.16.0.7/31) peers with 172.16.0.6 (AS 65100) and eth1 (172.16.0.11/31) peers with 172.16.0.10 (AS 65101); both neighbors are in Established state with prefixes received. The router’s RIB includes 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6 and 10.0.1.0/24 is locally connected on eth2, confirming proper routing.

End-to-end reachability also contradicts the report: pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) can ping each other successfully with 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT, showing the “other rack” is reachable through the fabric. This outcome is exactly what we expect when both leaf uplinks and the BGP sessions are up; traffic is being forwarded via the spine(s) without issue.

Localization: leaf_router_0_1 uplinks eth0 and eth1, BGP process (FRR) on leaf_router_0_1, and inter-rack path via spine routers. Root cause: the reported outage is not present; links and routing are operational—likely a transient or misobservation.

Corrective fix: No corrective action required. If the issue recurs, capture simultaneous evidence (interface state, BGP neighbor status, and host pings/traceroute) at the time of the incident to isolate any transient link or host-side problem.
